From: Muhammad Huda Nugroho <mhudan21@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] PCI: qcom: do not claim the native AER and PME services on
 IPQ5018

On IPQ5018 a CPU access to a downed PCIe link never completes - it hangs rather
than failing. That makes the two port services which walk config space when
something goes wrong unusable: when an endpoint drops its link (a 5G modem on
pcie1 does this on every subsystem restart) the AER service's aer_isr() ->
pci_walk_bus() -> dw_pcie_rd_other_conf() hangs the CPU instead of reporting an
error, and PME walks config space the same way on an error. On a CPE with a
1.5 s external watchdog, proven with pstore-ftrace, that hang is a silent reboot.

Clear host->native_aer and host->native_pme, which is what portdrv checks in
get_port_device_capability() unless pcie_ports=native overrides it, so those two
services are not instantiated on this host.

The flags have to be cleared in qcom_pcie_host_init() rather than after
dw_pcie_host_init() returns: that function calls pci_host_probe(), which is what
creates and binds the port service devices, so clearing them afterwards would be
too late. dw_pcie_host_init() allocates pp->bridge before invoking this callback,
so it is available there.

Scope this to IPQ5018 with its own cfg rather than to every platform sharing
ops_2_9_0. Those SoCs - IPQ6018, IPQ8074 Gen3, IPQ9574 - do all disable the PCIe
completion timeout in qcom_pcie_post_init_2_9_0(), which is a necessary
condition for this behaviour, but that is not evidence that their CPU-facing
interconnects handle a dead-link transaction the same way. Only IPQ5018 has been
shown to. Both of its hosts get the suppression, which is right for a host
limitation rather than a modem-only quirk.

This provides a scoped alternative to the pci=noaer and pcie_ports=compat kernel
arguments such a board otherwise needs, which disable AER machine-wide and put
every port service on every host into compat mode. It is not equivalent: compat
suppresses more than these two flags, pcie_ports=native overrides them, and the
remaining services stay eligible on their own terms. None of those performs the
AER source-discovery walk, but hotplug can enumerate and remove downstream
devices, so a board enabling it should look at this itself.

Signed-off-by: Muhammad Huda Nugroho <mhudan21@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-qcom.c
+++ b/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-qcom.c
@@ -258,6 +258,13 @@
 	bool override_no_snoop;
 	bool firmware_managed;
 	bool no_l0s;
+	/*
+	 * The port services that walk config space on an error (AER, PME)
+	 * cannot be used on this host: a CPU access to a downed link never
+	 * completes, so the walk hangs instead of failing. See
+	 * qcom_pcie_host_init().
+	 */
+	bool no_native_port_services;
 };
 
 struct qcom_pcie_port {
@@ -1293,6 +1300,33 @@
 	struct qcom_pcie *pcie = to_qcom_pcie(pci);
 	int ret;
 
+	/*
+	 * Keep the kernel from claiming the port services that walk config
+	 * space when something goes wrong. On this host a CPU access to a
+	 * downed link never completes, so when an endpoint drops the link the
+	 * AER service's aer_isr() -> pci_walk_bus() -> dw_pcie_rd_other_conf()
+	 * hangs the CPU rather than reporting an error, and PME walks config
+	 * space the same way on an error.
+	 *
+	 * This has to happen here rather than after dw_pcie_host_init()
+	 * returns: that function calls pci_host_probe(), which is what creates
+	 * and binds the port service devices, so clearing the flags afterwards
+	 * would be too late. dw_pcie_host_init() allocates pp->bridge before
+	 * invoking this callback, so it is available here.
+	 *
+	 * portdrv checks host->native_aer / native_pme in
+	 * get_port_device_capability(), unless pcie_ports=native overrides
+	 * them. Other services - hotplug, DPC, bandwidth control - stay
+	 * eligible on their own terms; none of them performs the AER
+	 * source-discovery walk, but hotplug can enumerate and remove
+	 * downstream devices, so a board that enables it wants its own look at
+	 * this.
+	 */
+	if (pcie->cfg->no_native_port_services) {
+		pp->bridge->native_aer = false;
+		pp->bridge->native_pme = false;
+	}
+
 	qcom_ep_reset_assert(pcie);
 
 	ret = pcie->cfg->ops->init(pcie);
@@ -1479,6 +1513,19 @@
 	.ops = &ops_2_9_0,
 };
 
+/*
+ * Same operations as cfg_2_9_0, but with the native AER/PME services
+ * suppressed. The dead-link hang this avoids has only been demonstrated on
+ * IPQ5018; the other SoCs sharing ops_2_9_0 also disable the PCIe completion
+ * timeout in qcom_pcie_post_init_2_9_0(), which is a necessary condition but
+ * not evidence that their CPU-facing interconnect behaves the same way, so they
+ * keep the default behaviour until someone shows otherwise on that hardware.
+ */
+static const struct qcom_pcie_cfg cfg_2_9_0_ipq5018 = {
+	.ops = &ops_2_9_0,
+	.no_native_port_services = true,
+};
+
 static const struct qcom_pcie_cfg cfg_sc8280xp = {
 	.ops = &ops_1_21_0,
 	.no_l0s = true,
@@ -2036,7 +2083,7 @@
 	{ .compatible = "qcom,pcie-apq8064", .data = &cfg_2_1_0 },
 	{ .compatible = "qcom,pcie-apq8084", .data = &cfg_1_0_0 },
 	{ .compatible = "qcom,pcie-ipq4019", .data = &cfg_2_4_0 },
-	{ .compatible = "qcom,pcie-ipq5018", .data = &cfg_2_9_0 },
+	{ .compatible = "qcom,pcie-ipq5018", .data = &cfg_2_9_0_ipq5018 },
 	{ .compatible = "qcom,pcie-ipq6018", .data = &cfg_2_9_0 },
 	{ .compatible = "qcom,pcie-ipq8064", .data = &cfg_2_1_0 },
 	{ .compatible = "qcom,pcie-ipq8064-v2", .data = &cfg_2_1_0 },
